**user**:
> We've discussed my stock prediction system before. I would now like to create a kind of genetic-algorithm-inspired population of prediction models, to make it easier to have continuous improvement. Here's the idea:
> 
> At any time, there are 10 algorithms in the pool. These are each a Julia script, function, package, or object (I'm not sure what would be best).
> 
> When it's time to run a prediction, all 10 algorithms are run. All of their individual predictions are recorded, and then when the actual values are observed, their success it measured.
> 
> We calculate scores for each algorithm, using my normal method used to weight different algorithms. Then (1) we randomly draw an algorithm weighted by the scores; (2) mutate it; (3) run it to make sure it completes successfully and store its results in our prediction file; (4) if it runs successfully, we use it to replace the lowest scoring algorithm.
> 
> I would also like to put a time constraint on the algorithms. Like 1 minute for now.
> 
> Before we implement this, do you have any thoughts on the design? By the way, this is actually the system I'm going to use with you, and you'll be the one proposing mutated algorithms.
**assistant**:
> This is a good framework, but I’d make it an **evolutionary online model-selection system** rather than immediately treating runtime success as fitness.
> 
> Key design choices:
> 
> 1. **Use isolated Julia scripts/processes** initially. Each model implements a tiny standard interface, e.g. `predict(context, nday) -> Dict{String, Float64}`. Run each in a fresh subprocess with a 60-second wall-clock timeout, memory limit if practical, and no network access. This prevents one model from contaminating state or crashing the pool.
> 
> 2. **Version everything.** Store each model’s source, parent model ID, mutation description, package/environment manifest, random seed, runtime, predictions, and code hash. A “model” is really an immutable version; pool slots point to versions.
> 
> 3. **Separate validity from quality.** A mutant that completes successfully is merely *eligible*. Replacing the worst incumbent immediately on successful execution will cause random drift toward untested models. Better:
>    - Create one challenger from a score-weighted parent.
>    - Validate it on historical walk-forward dates and current prediction generation.
>    - Give it a probationary slot or compare it against the weakest model over a fixed number of newly resolved prediction rounds.
>    - Replace only if its out-of-sample score clears a threshold.
> 
> 4. **Score on strictly pre-registered predictions.** Write predictions with an immutable timestamp before outcomes are known, then resolve them later. This is the central defense against accidental look-ahead and evolutionary overfitting.
> 
> 5. **Use smoothed, uncertainty-aware selection.** With only 10 models and noisy financial outcomes, raw scores can be unstable. Draw parents using a softened distribution—e.g. softmax of normalized rolling scores, with a nonzero exploration floor—rather than letting one recent winner monopolize reproduction.
> 
> 6. **Keep diversity deliberately.** Avoid ten variants of the same momentum model. Tag models by family/features/horizon and cap close relatives, or reserve 1–2 “exploration” slots. Preserve a permanent archive of retired strong models so evolution cannot forget useful approaches.
> 
> 7. **Maintain an untouched evaluation stream.** Since I will propose mutations, repeated adaptation to the same historical test set is a real risk. Use walk-forward validation for development, but retain a delayed or quarantined evaluation period for promotion decisions.
> 
> I would start with **10 slots: 8 incumbents, 1 challenger/probationer, 1 protected baseline**. The protected baseline might be a simple regularized momentum/mean-reversion ensemble; it ensures the system always has a sanity benchmark.
> 
> For mutation, I can propose structured changes—feature additions, parameter changes, alternative loss functions, horizon blending, regime gates, or ensemble combinations—rather than arbitrary code edits. This should make evolution interpretable and auditable.
