> Yes: allow candidates to join the live pool and earn their score prospectively. With news or other live feeds, that is more honest than pretending a new model can be fairly backtested.
> 
> A few refinements:
> 
> - **Internet access and isolation are compatible.** Run each model in a separate process/container with a 60-second deadline, while permitting outbound access. Log request URLs, timestamps, responses (or hashes/snapshots where feasible), and prediction cutoff time. This is mainly for auditability and avoiding accidental future-data leakage.
> 
> - **Candidates should not immediately displace incumbents.** Let the pool be `10 + N_candidates`, but cap $N$ (perhaps 2–5) to control compute. A candidate becomes eligible for replacement only after, say, 20 resolved forecast instances or an effective sample-size threshold.
> 
> - **Your score is a sensible proper-ish interval score** if each algorithm emits several distinct price draws: it treats those draws as a lognormal predictive distribution and rewards probability assigned to the realized $[\mathrm{low},\mathrm{high}]$ interval. Require models to emit, e.g., 20–100 draws rather than merely five, ideally generated reproducibly from a recorded seed.
> 
> - **But this is not itself uncertainty-aware model selection.** The exponential age term smooths/discounts old performance; it does not account for the fact that a candidate with 3 observations is much less known than an incumbent with 300. New models need shrinkage toward a prior/baseline score or an explicit exploration bonus.
> 
> - **Do not use raw `skill` as sampling weights.** Log scores are generally negative. Convert a standardized rolling score to probabilities, for example:
>   \[
>   p_m \propto \epsilon + \exp\left((\bar{s}_m + b_m)/\tau\right),
>   \]
>   where $b_m$ is an uncertainty/exploration adjustment and $\tau$ controls selection concentration.
> 
> - **Keep horizon separate.** Your intermediate score groups by `ahead`, but `results2` drops it and sums across horizons. That may be intended, but I would generally select model variants by horizon (or use explicit horizon weights), since a good 1-day model and good 7-day model can differ.
> 
> - **Review the `npreds` normalization.** `npreds=sum(npreds)/5` and then `logskill / npreds` appears to make score depend on the number of sampled draws in a somewhat accidental way. Score each resolved forecast once; aggregate forecast scores with recency weights. The number of draws should affect distribution estimation, not grant a model more or less evolutionary fitness.
> 
> I would define a candidate as a normal immutable `AlgorithmVersion`, give it a new ID and parent/mutation metadata, run it live from birth onward, and promote it when its shrunk prospective score beats the weakest eligible incumbent.
